t": 4.0, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 15.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 6.85, "baseline_margin": 12.4, "analyst_adjustment": 0.4, "key_factors": ["Colorado's D+6 lean and Democratic hold on statewide office favor Weiser in the open seat", "All seven expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and markets price a Democratic win near 97%", "No general-election polling exists, so the forecast rests on fundamentals and ratings", "Marx matches Weiser in fundraising but is burning cash quickly and skipped the first debate"], "rationale": "Colorado's partisan lean, a unanimous Solid/Safe Democratic expert consensus, and betting markets near 97% all point to Weiser holding the seat for the party, with Marx's even fundraising undercut by rapid spending and a low statewide profile. The complete absence of polling keeps uncertainty elevated but not enough to move the race out of Safe D.", "watch": "Any late general-election polling showing Marx within single digits, or a major Weiser misstep in the remaining debates, would be the first sign of a genuine contest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governorship is open as Jared Polis terms out, and Phil Weiser carries the Democratic banner against Republican Victor Marx. The state's D+6 lean and its recent Democratic track record in statewide races frame the contest, though an open seat removes the incumbent's personal vote from the equation.\n\nNo general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on other signals: every major race rater calls Colorado Solid or Safe Democratic, and betting markets put Democratic win odds near 97%. Fundraising is roughly even, but Marx is spending his money quickly and skipped the first debate, while Weiser has shared stages with independent Greg Lopez. Third-party and independent cand